  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 AM MDT Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nToday through Tue night...The upper level pattern has a trough \nto the north over western WA this morning and a closed low over \nnorthern CA, both in alignment longitudinally. By this \nafternoon, this pair will be at the WA-ID border in the north \nand over Las Vegas to the south. Weak upper level flow with \nabundant moisture as it gets close should develop from this, \nwith moderate and locally heavy rainfall amounts, with snow \nabove 7500ft elevation during the day. By midnight, this trough-\nlow pair has exited the Gem State and a north wind develops \nover the forecast area. This will shut off precipitation, save\nfor where the northerly to northwest surface wind can find an\nupslope condition, such as in the eastern and southeastern\nhighlands.\n\nContinued cloud cover will cool temperatures some more today\ncompared to Sat, then clearer skies in the west on Mon will\nallow temperatures in the western areas to warm at least a\nlittle. On the eastern side, temperatures will continue to cool\ndue to the cold air mass moving in due to the northerly wind. On\nTue, depending on fog formation, nearly all areas will see a\nleveling off of the previous day's warming or a warming trend. \nOvernight lows plunge to the teens and 20s once the clouds are \ngone on Tue night, except in the eastern Magic Valley where they\nshould hover around freezing.\n\nModerate to strong wind possible in the wake of the trough,\nespecially in the mountains on Mon and to a lesser extent in the\nSnake River plain tonight.\n\nWed through Sat...Even late Tue night the individual\ndeterministic model runs differ on the timing of the next\ntrough, with the NAM already developing precipitation at the end\nof the short term period above. But most have it returning to\nthe highlands that border UT and WY on Wed. Only one cluster of\n9 percent of the ensemble runs have no upper level troughing\nover Idaho. The upper level zonal pattern keeps the forecast\nhighly uncertain in timeing for Thu and Fri, while Sat in all\nclusters appears to be dry. However, changeability in the\nforeast is highest here in autumn. One thing that can be said is\nthat there are no major upper level troughs making an approach\nto the US northwest coast during this period.",
